10. Majority Versus Non-Majority Social Choice comparison arises from Condorcet Jury Theorem (recent proof of Condorcet Jury Theorem in the context of Strength of Weak Learnability - Majority Voting in Learning theory - AdaBoost Ensemble Classifier - https://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.03153.pdf ) and Margulis-Russo Threshold phenomenon in Boolean Social Choice i.e how individual decision correctness affects group decision correctness. Equating the two social choices has enormous implications for Complexity theory because all complexity classes are subsumed by Majority-VoterSAT boolean function composition. Depth-2 majority (Majority+Majority composition) social choice function - boolean and non-boolean - is an instance of Axiom of Choice (AOC) stated as "for any collection of nonempty sets X, there exists a function f such that f(A) is in A, for all A in X". Depth-2 majority (both boolean and non-boolean voters set-partition induced by candidate voted for), which is the conventional democracy, chooses one element per constituency electorate set A of set of constituencies X in the leaves, at Depth-1. Conventional parliamentary democracy is an obvious and direct application of Axiom of Choice defined as "For any set X of nonempty sets, there exists a choice function f that is defined on X and maps each set of X to an element of that set" (definition source - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice#Equivalents ) which in the context of democracies could be rephrased as "For any set X of constituencies of electorate, there exists a social choice function f that is defined on X and maps each constituency electorate of X to a representative of that constituency" and earlier definition implies a well-ordering (a least element) or a uniquely chosen candidate within each subset. In other words Axiom of Choice(AOC) guarantees the existence of democratized electoral process subject to well-ordering or ranking of electorate based on some criteria. Usual criterion of maximum votes in constituency as qualifier is the democracy choice function defining well-ordering which could be violated when two or more candidates within a constituency garner equal number of votes and there is a tie. Example: In a parliament of 2 constituencies, 10 electors per constituency and 3 contestants per constituency, following partition is well-ordered in constituency1 and tied in constituency2: Contestant1-Contestant2-Contestant3
