Earlier counterexample requires the votes for candidates in democracies to be strict partitions which have no repetitive parts - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partition_function_(number_theory)#Strict_partition_function . Allowing self-votes by candidates (candidates usually vote for themselves) sometimes prevents strict partitions - an example scenario of tie-free election by recursive binary strict partition is described in section 1627. Axiom of Choice, HORNSAT for multiple choice Question-Answering, Subjective Question-Answering in which an answer is chosen for a question from set of all possible answers and Social choice have been shown to be mutually equivalent in sections 1627 and 1629. Following freak scenario of win by candidate1 through self-voting is theoretically possible - For 33 electors-3 candidates example: if only 31 of 33 electors vote, candidate2 and candidate3 abstain without self-voting, candidate1 wins by voting for self though in reality it is a tie between 3 candidates: Candidate1 - 11 (10 other voters + 1 self) Candidate2 - 10 (10 other voters - no self-vote) Candidate3 - 10 (10 other voters - no self-vote)
