1389. (THEORY and FEATURE) Conceptual Graph of Theory aligned to Features of NeuronRain - 2
Intrinsic Merit Ranking can be defined as a MAXSAT problem. Random matrix based LSMR/LSQR SAT solver approximately solves MAXSAT in polynomial time on an average. Ranking of texts based on distance similarity is also a problem solved by collision-supportive Locality Sensitive Hashing - similar texts are clustered in a bucket chain. Question-Answering/Interview Intrinsic Merit is a QBFSAT problem (in IP=PSPACE). Question-Answering problem could be formulated as Linear or Polynomial Threshold Function in Learning theory perspective that weighs answer to each question as variable in LTF-PTF. Conventionally Question-Answering has been limited to open-ended questions while in most academic examination environments scope of answer is limited by Multiple Choice Questions having constant number of answer choices. Equivalence between Adaptive Multiple Choice Question-Answering admission tests (e.g GRE,TOEFL) and some games (viz., Chess,Go for which PSPACE-completeness has been proved by reduction from Generalized Geography) is evident from the fact that ( ) every multiple choice question has constant number of answer choices and next question depends on answer to previous question - that can be equated to - ( ) every move in a game that has a countermove by opponent. Debate is a natural extension of Question-Answering that makes Questioning and Answering a multilateral peer-to-peer bidirectional process: Every participant in a debate can ask questions to other participants (or) answer questions by other participants. In other words Debate is a harder problem than Question-Answering - for example imaginary variant of Cricket - PRICKET - proposed and implemented in NeuronRain allows simultaneous bowling-batting roles to both teams which can be equated to a Debate where both participants ask questions or answer questions by the other. Cricket in its present format simulates sequence of Questions (bowling) posed by team1 that are Answered (batting) by team2 and team2 has to wait for its turn till team1 finishes all questions which is parallelized in PRICKET (every question by team1 is immediately countered by team2 in next ball). Generative AI models have so far concentrated only on asymmetric Question-Answering where AI answers human questions but not yet on AI-Human debate. NeuronRain theory drafts investigate error bounds of Multiple Choice Question-Answering (MCQ) and its Linear Threshold Function formalism in 2 special contexts: (1) Academic Multiple Choice Questions in competitive examinations (2) DRONA theoretical Electronic Voting Machines that extend conventional buttons for candidate choices to an MCQ questionnaire answered by voter on why a candidate is chosen (i.e every democratic election in MCQ EVM model is equivalent to an academic examination for the voters in MCQ format - Difference is: Candidates succeed instead of voters who take the exam). Quoting section 1648 of https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/blob/master/asfer-docs/AstroInferDesign.txt , ".... If each question in MCQ has m answer choices and MCQ contains n questions, worst case probability of voter error for all n questions = (m-1)^n/m^n ...." which is tantamount to definition and Peres theorem bounds for noise sensitivity of linear threshold functions for m=2 in https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~odonnell/boolean-analysis/lecture19.pdf - ".... We recapitulate the definition of noise sensitivity. If f : {−1, 1}^n → {−1, 1}, then for 0 ≤ epsilon ≤ 1/2, NSepsilon(f) = Prx,y[f(x) != f(y)]. .... Theorem 2.3 (Peres ’04) Let f : {−1, 1}^n → {−1, 1} be an LTF. Then NSepsilon(f) ≤ 2√epsilon....". In other words, a voter or student errs in answering all n questions of binary choice (m=2) with worst case exact probability 1/2^n (probability of wrong answer=epsilon=1/2 per question) which can be compared to Peres theorem worst case bound for noise sensitivity of an LTF for epsilon=1/2 (or each binary answer variable bit in LTF can be flipped to be 50% wrong): For epsilon=1/2, 1/2^n (MCQ exact error bound) <= 2*sqrt(1/2) (Peres theorem upperbound) or 1/2^n <= sqrt(2) which is a huge upperbound for an exact error bound of MCQ. Noise sensitivity of an MCQ-LTF in an examination or voting context could be construed as overall probability of a student or voter deviating from correct answer choices for n questions. This error later becomes per voter competence in Condorcet Jury Theorem. Pseudorandom Choice is a Non-Majority Social Choice Function Voter SAT can be of any complexity class - 3SAT, QBFSAT etc., Space Filling by circles is a vast area of research - Circle Packing. Parallel Circle Packing unifies three fields - Parallel Pseudorandom Generators (classical or quantum PRGs - ordinates on 2-D plane are generated in parallel and at random which is underneath most natural processes - including but not limited to Rain, Teapot Shards, Agriculture), 0-1 Integer Linear Programming and Circle Packing. Efficient parallel circle packing has computational geometric importance - geometric search where each circle is a query which might contain expected point - planar point location. Random Close Packing and Circle Packing are Constraint Satisfaction/SAT Problems. Polynomial packing which generalizes circle packing to arbitrary closed curves sparsely or closely packed on a surface finds applications in GIS analytics of Urban sprawl contour polynomials embedded on a space forming a finite multiply connected region - https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/simply-connected-region . Closely packed Urban sprawl contour polynomials can be approximated by Voronoi diagram tessellation polygons (follows from Jordan curve theorem and Weirstrass theorem for approximation of a function by polynomials)
Intrinsic Merit Ranking can be defined as a MAXSAT problem. Random matrix based LSMR/LSQR SAT solver approximately solves MAXSAT in polynomial time on an average. Ranking of texts based on distance similarity is also a problem solved by collision-supportive Locality Sensitive Hashing - similar texts are clustered in a bucket chain. Question-Answering/Interview Intrinsic Merit is a QBFSAT problem (in IP=PSPACE). Question-Answering problem could be formulated as Linear or Polynomial Threshold Function in Learning theory perspective that weighs answer to each question as variable in LTF-PTF. Conventionally Question-Answering has been limited to open-ended questions while in most academic examination environments scope of answer is limited by Multiple Choice Questions having constant number of answer choices. Equivalence between Adaptive Multiple Choice Question-Answering admission tests (e.g GRE,TOEFL) and some games (viz., Chess,Go for which PSPACE-completeness has been proved by reduction from Generalized Geography) is evident from the fact that ( ) every multiple choice question has constant number of answer choices and next question depends on answer to previous question - that can be equated to - ( ) every move in a game that has a countermove by opponent. Debate is a natural extension of Question-Answering that makes Questioning and Answering a multilateral peer-to-peer bidirectional process: Every participant in a debate can ask questions to other participants (or) answer questions by other participants. In other words Debate is a harder problem than Question-Answering - for example imaginary variant of Cricket - PRICKET - proposed and implemented in NeuronRain allows simultaneous bowling-batting roles to both teams which can be equated to a Debate where both participants ask questions or answer questions by the other. Cricket in its present format simulates sequence of Questions (bowling) posed by team1 that are Answered (batting) by team2 and team2 has to wait for its turn till team1 finishes all questions which is parallelized in PRICKET (every question by team1 is immediately countered by team2 in next ball). Generative AI models have so far concentrated only on asymmetric Question-Answering where AI answers human questions but not yet on AI-Human debate. NeuronRain theory drafts investigate error bounds of Multiple Choice Question-Answering (MCQ) and its Linear Threshold Function formalism in 2 special contexts: (1) Academic Multiple Choice Questions in competitive examinations (2) DRONA theoretical Electronic Voting Machines that extend conventional buttons for candidate choices to an MCQ questionnaire answered by voter on why a candidate is chosen (i.e every democratic election in MCQ EVM model is equivalent to an academic examination for the voters in MCQ format - Difference is: Candidates succeed instead of voters who take the exam). Quoting section 1648 of https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/blob/master/asfer-docs/AstroInferDesign.txt , ".... If each question in MCQ has m answer choices and MCQ contains n questions, worst case probability of voter error for all n questions = (m-1)^n/m^n ...." which is tantamount to definition and Peres theorem bounds for noise sensitivity of linear threshold functions for m=2 in https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~odonnell/boolean-analysis/lecture19.pdf - ".... We recapitulate the definition of noise sensitivity. If f : {−1, 1}^n → {−1, 1}, then for 0 ≤ epsilon ≤ 1/2, NSepsilon(f) = Prx,y[f(x) != f(y)]. .... Theorem 2.3 (Peres ’04) Let f : {−1, 1}^n → {−1, 1} be an LTF. Then NSepsilon(f) ≤ 2√epsilon....". In other words, a voter or student errs in answering all n questions of binary choice (m=2) with worst case exact probability 1/2^n (probability of wrong answer=epsilon=1/2 per question) which can be compared to Peres theorem worst case bound for noise sensitivity of an LTF for epsilon=1/2 (or each binary answer variable bit in LTF can be flipped to be 50% wrong): For epsilon=1/2, 1/2^n (MCQ exact error bound) <= 2*sqrt(1/2) (Peres theorem upperbound) or 1/2^n <= sqrt(2) which is a huge upperbound for an exact error bound of MCQ. Noise sensitivity of an MCQ-LTF in an examination or voting context could be construed as overall probability of a student or voter deviating from correct answer choices for n questions. This error later becomes per voter competence in Condorcet Jury Theorem. Pseudorandom Choice is a Non-Majority Social Choice Function Voter SAT can be of any complexity class - 3SAT, QBFSAT etc., Space Filling by circles is a vast area of research - Circle Packing. Parallel Circle Packing unifies three fields - Parallel Pseudorandom Generators (classical or quantum PRGs - ordinates on 2-D plane are generated in parallel and at random which is underneath most natural processes - including but not limited to Rain, Teapot Shards, Agriculture), 0-1 Integer Linear Programming and Circle Packing. Efficient parallel circle packing has computational geometric importance - geometric search where each circle is a query which might contain expected point - planar point location. Random Close Packing and Circle Packing are Constraint Satisfaction/SAT Problems. Polynomial packing which generalizes circle packing to arbitrary closed curves sparsely or closely packed on a surface finds applications in GIS analytics of Urban sprawl contour polynomials embedded on a space forming a finite multiply connected region - https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/simply-connected-region . Closely packed Urban sprawl contour polynomials can be approximated by Voronoi diagram tessellation polygons (follows from Jordan curve theorem and Weirstrass theorem for approximation of a function by polynomials)
