Transformers are recent advances in Text analytics - NeuronRain Textgraph implementations for Recursive Lambda Function Growth and Named Entity Recognition extend transformers to textgraph vertices degree attention for inferring importance of word vertices of textgraphs.A Question-Answering Bot has been implemented in NeuronRain which takes natural language questions from users and queries wikipedia corpus for answer summary to create a rephrased deep-learnt natural language answer by WordNet walk on edges chosen based on top percentile Transformers Degree attention Query-Key-Values from wikipedia summary textgraph. Recursive Lambda Function Growth implementation additionally supports a fill-in-the-blanks prediction of missing words in a text by textgraph path degree attention that could be well suited also for predicting missing vowels in vowelless text (de)compression. Question-Answering Bot also implements a minimal Formal Large Languge Model Nachiketas based on XTAG rule tree families that synthesizes a phrase or sentence from PoS tagged random walk over textgraph (and SpaCy-REBEL-PrimeKG knowledge graph) with some blanks for conjunctions and adpositions which can be restored either by fill_in_the_blanks() implementation in RecursiveLambdaFunctionGrowth.py (section 1455) or by passing a flattened inorder traversal of CCG-CFG-PCFG-XTAG rule tree as dict object argument to the Bot function. Meaningfulness of the sentences synthesized by Formal LLM is quantified by perplexity scores - a counterexample sentence on less meaningful but grammatically correct sentence is mentioned in 803. Semantic relationship between words in a synthesized sentence could also be estimated by WordNet distances (Perl implementation - https://metacpan.org/pod/WordNet ::Similarity by [Ted Pedersen] which is also part of NLTK). NeuronRain implements a proprietary wordnet perplexity measure for sentences. Phrases synthesized by textgraph randomwalks could be classified as Noun phrases (NP) and Verb phrases (VP) that could be combined to form a Phrase Structure Grammar (PSG) sentence - Example: PSG parse tree for a sentence in https://www.nltk.org/book/ch08.html - Diagram 8. Recursive Lambda Function Growth algorithm implemented in NeuronRain that composes a lambda function tree from randomwalks on textgraphs has striking resemblance to Frege Dependency Grammar which models every sentence as a graph of edge relations between head word to dependent words - sentence synthesis in LLMs is the opposite process of inferring a parse tree from a sentence - sentences could be synthesized in Frege dependency logic by identifying edge relationships between words in textgraph randomwalks and linear concatenations of those dependency relations (which might output a jumbled out of order sentence). Formal LLM implementation in NeuronRain also autogenerates sentence PoS templates from inorder traversals of grammar trees extracted from Penn Treebank datasets which are pigeonholed in-situ by tokens of textgraph randomwalks - sentence PoS template is filled to the maximum and unfilled PoS tag slots are filled later by permuting possible words in a corpus for those unfilled PoS tags. Earlier LLM algorithm creates grammatically correct Chomsky sentences from which a subset of meaningful sentences have to be chosen - Meaningfulness in statistical LLMs is measured by perplexity while meaningfulness in Carnap and Wittgenstein formal logic is defined as satisfiability of first order logic(FOL) encoding of natural language sentences (First order logic satisfiability is undecidable though there are decidable fragments of FOL). Formal meaningfulness is qualitative binary - True or False value for FOL encoding of sentence - while statistical meaningfulness by perplexity is quantitative (can be ranked). Nachiketas Formal LLM implements an alternative algorithm for universal sentence synthesis by directly filling PoS slots of grammar tree inorder traversed templates without resorting to textgraph randomwalks which enumerates all possible Chomsky English sentences, a set of exponential size, and meaningful sentences form an infinitesimal subset of Chomsky sentences. Datasources for Medical knowledge graphs (TDC, PrimeKG) have been integrated into Formal LLM which could be an AI assistant for medical diagnosis and drug discovery through drug-disease link prediction algorithms (in most cases link might be missing and has to be heuristically predicted - NetworkX graph link prediction algorithms - https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/link_prediction.html - [Soundararajan-Hopcroft] link prediction - https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.link_prediction.cn_soundarajan_hopcroft.html ). (1) Recursive Lambda Function Growth and (2) Inorder traversal of grammar PoS trees to get flattened sentence templates are tree evaluation problems that evaluate respectively, ( ) the lambda function composition tree and ( ) the sentence at the root from internal PoS grammar tree nodes, which has recently gained importance due to ( ) [Cook-Mertz] theorem - https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2023/174/ - proving tree evaluation can be accomplished in O(logN loglogN) space and ( ) [Ryan Williams] - https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/017/ - Time t computation can be simulated in sqrt(t) space, thereby making sentence synthesis space-efficient an important concern for large language models occupying huge space. Formal LLM and Formal AI depend purely on linguistics and formal language theory and diverge from conventional Statistical LLMs and Statistical AI which are corpora-intensive and require pretrained neural network models, by viewing the concept of sentence meaningulness from the angle of graph theory and linguistics - especially two prominent schools of sanskrit grammar exist due to ( ) Yaska advocating atomism or words giving meaning to sentences (Nirukta school of etymology - author of Nighantu - https://ia801504.us.archive.org/1/items/in.ernet.dli.2015.537219/2015.537219.yaskas-nirukta_text.pdf ) and (*) Panini's holism or connections (sandhi) between words giving meaning to sentences (Vyakarana school - author of Ashtadhyayi - https://vedicheritage.gov.in/flipbook/Ashtadhyayi_Panini_SCV_Vol_I/#book/ ) - NeuronRain implements two formal measures of meaning of a sentence - (1) Core meaningfulness from core numbers of words in textgraph of sentence - weightage is accorded to important word vertices of high core numbers - inspired by Atomism (2) WordNet path meaningful from wordnet connectivity of the words constituting the sentence - weightage is accorded to connections between words - inspired by Holism. Nachiketas Formal LLM implements an optional sentence synthesizer that depends on Ngrams.dev V3 Google Ngram dataset to get a sentence by connecting textgraph randomwalks or completing a semi-filled grammar tree PoS sentence template by wildcard ngram queries. Part-of-speech verification for synthesized sentences ideally requires an exhaustive search of matching PoS sequence in inorder traversals of Penn Treebank grammar trees. Due to this limitation an alternative PoS verification that distinguishes DisplaCy dependency graphs of sentences having correct and incorrect PoS structure through some graph attribute has been envisaged.
