      "score": 1.0,
      "text_matches": [
        {
          "object_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/214814bf57ff57eb4d7293ec7d5c0fc477f68c2e",
          "object_type": "CommitMessage",
          "property": "message",
          "fragment": "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1668. (THEORY and FEATURE) Software Analytics - Kernel Analytics and Scheduler Analytics - Enhanced Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) Observability - runqlat and runqlen added to bpftrace script - related to 561,1191,619,770,906,911,771,1192,1443,1459,1488 and all sections on Program Analysis, AsFer-USBmd-VIRGO-KingCobra FTrace kernel callgraph analysis - Valgrind-Callgrind-KCacheGrind-Flamegraph userspace callgraph analysis, SATURN control flow analysis of VIRGO linux kernel, Software Analytics, Pandemic model of Botnet-Defense adversarial Game theoretic security, Cybercrime analytics, Anti-Malware,Anti-Ransomware,Anti-Theft and Anti-Plagiarism tools, KML, LAKE,Intrusion Prevention-Detection Systems(IDS and IPS),Anomaly Detection - 4 June 2026\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1668.1 python-src/software_analytics/bpftrace.sh has been updated to enable BPF debug traces and include runqlat.bt and runqlen.bt that respectively trace CPU runqueue latency and runqueue lengths - they are invoked by following bpfcc commands:\n\trunqlat-bpfcc 1 1\n\trunqlen-bpfcc 1 1\n1688.2 Logs testlogs/bpftrace.log.4June2026 show peak runqueue latency at 8-15 and 32-63 microseconds ranges and runqueue length 386:\n     usecs               : count     distribution\n         8 -> 15         : 64       |****************************************|\n\t\t...\n        32 -> 63         : 46       |****************************            |\n     runqlen       : count     distribution\n        0          : 396      |****************************************|\n1688.3 Accurate per-CPU runqueue BPF statistics are essential for theoretical Survival Index OS scheduler which is a dynamic hashmap of <timeout>----<list of threads-processes> envisaged in section 751 of https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/Grafit/blob/master/course_material/NeuronRain/AdvancedComputerScienceAndMachineLearning/AdvancedComputerScienceAndMachineLearning.txt. Runqlat and Runqlen are one step closer to this requirement which provide consolidated number of processes per CPU but not the list of process IDs.\n1688.4 eBPF observable traces produced in testlogs/bpftrace.log.4June2026 could be translated to /etc/kernel_analytics.conf variables exported kernelwide as AI analytics with additional preprocessing (section 1459.4)\n\nReferences:\n----------\n1688.5 eBPF observability and modern Linux - https://www.brendangregg.com/Slides/UM2019_BPF_a_new_type_of_software.pdf - Transition from (*) Binary userspace applications-kernelspace system calls to (*) Ternary userspace applications-kernelspace system calls-eBPF JIT compiler and helper calls - Netflix eBPF case study - \"... >150k AWS EC2 Ubuntu server instances, ~34% US Internet traffic at night, >130M subscribers, ~14 active BPF programs on every instance (so far) ...\"\n1688.6 Process anomaly detection by eBPF traces and autoencoder - https://www.evilsocket.net/2022/08/15/Process-behaviour-anomaly-detection-using-eBPF-and-unsupervised-learning-Autoencoders/ - In earlier example, eBPF traces in testlogs/bpftrace.log.4June2026 could be compressed and decompressed by encoder-decoder implementation in https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/Grafit/blob/master/course_material/NeuronRain/AdvancedComputerScienceAndMachineLearning/code/Autoencoders.py to decipher anomalies.",
