      "text_matches": [
        {
          "object_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/3f193cf35f0655fbf64fdd23239ef7426fc98f41",
          "object_type": "CommitMessage",
          "property": "message",
          "fragment": "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1629. (THEORY and FEATURE) Computational Geometric Integer Factorization - Python 3.13 + Spark 4.0.0 + OpenJDK 17 + Linux Kernel 6.14 Upgraded Single node clustering benchmark - related to 498,601,1226,1259,1303,1319,1321,1323,1324,1325,1355,1365,1366,1369,1379,1381,1384,1385,1401,1419,1454,1487 and all sections on Integer Factorization (DMRC MapReduce PySpark and NC HPC Rust implementations),Computational Geometry,Set Partitions-to-Tile Cover reduction by Lagranges Four Square Theorem and ILP,Primality Testing,RSA Semiprimes,Almost-Square Bounding Boxes in Large Scale Visual Recognition,Pell's Equation,Brahmagupta's Chakravala,Approximate Factoring by Newton-Raphson Rayshooting queries,Algebraic Geometry of Computational Geometric Factorization,Quantum computation and Derandomization of BQP Factorization by Condorcet Jury Theorem Majority-BQPVoterSAT boolean composition,Integer partitions,Diophantine representation,Circuit Value Problem - 5 August 2025\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1629.1 Computational Geometric Factorization Python-Spark MapReduce implementation has been benchmarked on upgraded Python3.13 + Spark 4.0.0 + OpenJDK 17 Java binary configuration for 17 bits integer 99129 by following commandline:\n../../../../spark-4.0.0-bin-hadoop3/bin/spark-submit DiscreteHyperbolicFactorizationUpperbound_TileSearch_Optimized.py 99129 1 False False all False 2> testlogs/DiscreteHyperbolicFactorizationUpperbound_TileSearch_Optimized.Python3.13Spark4.0.0Upgrade.log.5August2025\n1629.2 Factors found for 99129 are (from python-src/DiscreteHyperbolicFactorizationUpperbound_TileSearch_Optimized.factors):\n{\"99129\": [\"519\", \"573\", \"33043\", \"191\", \"3\", \"173\"]}\n1629.3 Earlier benchmark significantly alters the python-java interaction because all earlier benchmarks were based on legacy JDK 1.8.0_144. All factors of 99129 were found in 6.1 seconds (from nanoseconds/10^9 in testlogs/DiscreteHyperbolicFactorizationUpperbound_TileSearch_Optimized.Python3.13Spark4.0.0Upgrade.log.5August2025):\nnanoseconds elapsed so far in finding all factors:  6167543990\n\nReferences:\n-----------\n1629.4 Circuit Value Problem (CVP) - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_value_problem - Known to be P-Complete which means an NC parallel algorithm for CVP proves NC=P. Boolean Value Problem which is the special case of CVP when circuit is a tree has been shown to be complete for NC^1. If NC=P, Primality testing is in NC. HORNSAT is another known P-complete problem and section 1627 of Grafit/course_material/NeuronRain/AdvancedComputerScienceAndMachineLearning/AdvancedComputerScienceAndMachineLearning.txt discusses connections between Axiom of Choice, Social Choice, Multiple-choice Question-Answering and HORNSAT eventually making all 4 to be equivalent. Proof of NC=P could have sweeping effect on mathematics because Axiom of Choice being the foundational assumption of mathematics is P-Complete in its HORNSAT-Multiple Choice Q&A formulation. Circuit Value Problem for factorizaation has been used as VoterSAT gadget in section 1152 of https://shrinivaasanka.github.io/Krishna_iResearch_DoxygenDocs/ in which every voter factors same integer N by a BQP quantum algorithm and votes Yes or No if factors are correct through a multiplication circuit checking the factor values and by definition of BQP and Condorcet Jury Theorem all voters decide with 67% (BQP succeeds 2/3 for yes instances) correctness leading to an asymptotically 100% correct factorization or derandomization of BQP (or quantum superposition collapses to a classical state asymptotically for infinite voters).\n1629.5 What Every Computer Scientist Needs to Know About Parallelization - https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.03647v1 - Survey of Parallel computing models from legacy NC-PRAM-BSP-LogP to modern Multicore-GPU paradigms and an example of road traffic simulation.",
