://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/deployments"\n      },\n      "score": 1.0,\n      "text_matches": [\n        {\n          "object_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/b57544ec508307a1496d69a87d12885095da3da0",\n          "object_type": "CommitMessage",\n          "property": "message",\n          "fragment": "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1591. (THEORY and FEATURE) Analysis of Matiyasevich Python programs for Riemann Hypothesis 4 - AI Loop termination analysis through various models, D\'Alembert convergence test for loop condition LHS-RHS ratio timeseries and increased iterations - related to 629,751,1191,1217,1354,1357,1443,1459,1477,1552,1553,1554,1556 to all sections on Riemann Zeta Function, Set partitions, Computational geometry, Program analysis/Software analytics/Scheduler Analytics,Master Theorem,Busy Beaver Function, Fractal Hausdorff Dimension-Box counting dimension, Timeseries analysis ,Complement diophantines, Worst case execution time estimation  - 24 March 2025\\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1591.1 python-src/MatiyasevichRH.py has been updated to increase number of iterations to 20000.\\n1591.2 D\'Alembert series convergence test for while loop LHS-RHS ratio timeseries (present term/previous term) has been implemented.\\n1591.3 In continuation of Grok 3 X AI Think reasoning engine loop termination analysis of Matiyasevich python program for Riemann Hypothesis in Section 1556.11 - https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/blob/master/python-src/testlogs/Grok%203%20Think%20-%20Reasoning%20Engine%20termination%20analysis%20of%20Matiyasevich%20python%20program%20for%20Riemann%20Hypothesis.pdf - which concludes that the while loop is infinite and thus proving Riemann Hypothesis, few additional AI models - ChatGPT, Meta AI Llama, Claude AI, DeepSeek, Mistral AI - have been consulted and their analysis has been summarized in https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/blob/c9e6d7ab3d47142c8223037ea9c2ece1c9090965/python-src/testlogs/AILoopTerminationAnalysisOfMatiyasevichPythonProgramForRiemannHypothesisByVariousAIModels.pdf\\n1591.4 Most AI models are circumspect in their analysis of the while loop condition and predict the while loop to be infinite with high probability due to exponential growth of RHS and occasional increment of LHS due to GCD computation not being able to catch-up with RHS.\\n1591.5 Iterations have been increased to 20000 to verify if the Higuchi Fractal Dimension of the while loop condition LHS-RHS ratio series reaches 2 in which case the set could be a Mandelbrot fractal set undecidable by Blum-Shub-Smale (BSS) Turing machines over complex field per section 1554.7 on undecidability of Mandelbrot sets by BSS machines and therefore by less powerful binary Turing machines - If this timeseries ever reaches a fractal dimension of 2 after huge iterations, it makes entire program undecidable possibly due to an infinite loop thus making Riemann Hypothesis to be True.\\n1591.6 If, on the contrary, above while loop terminates, then from section 1217.14 on [Matiyasevich-O\'Rear-Aaronson], earlier Matiyasevich python program is indeed a Turing machine of 744 states that halts \\"if and only if\\" Riemann Hypothesis is False (due to if and only if clause)- in other words, if above python program has a Turing machine of states more or less than 744, then Riemann Hypothesis is possibly True. So an alternative could be to encode earlier python program to a minimum Turing machine in NQL and check whether number of states in it is 744.\\n1591.7 Logs and graph plot for while loop LHS-RHS ratio are available in:\\n\\tMatiyasevichRH.LHSRHSRatio.24March2025.jpg\\n\\tMatiyasevichRH.greppedlog.24March2025 (grepped for Higuchi dimension and D\'Alembert test due to huge 1.7 GB sized log = full log withheld)\\nFrom above logs, Higuchi Fractal dimension seems to be hovering around ~1.8 and the graph plot for while loop LHS-RHS ratio timeseries has a striking resemblance to Robert May\'s logistic map x(n+1) = r*x(n)(1-x(n)) - https://blbadger.github.io/logistic-map.html - possibly predicting chaos underneath Riemann Zeta Function. D\'Alembert ratio test doesn\'t show convergence for 20000 iterations due to fluctuating but attenuating ratio.",\n          "matches": [\n            {\n              "text": "THEORY and FEATURE",\n              "indices": [\n                135,\n                153\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                348,\n                351\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "shrinivaasanka",\n              "indices": [\n                1314,\n                1328\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                1568,\n                1571\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                1710,\n                1713\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "shrinivaasanka",\n              "indices": [\n                1771,\n                1785\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                2048,\n                2051\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                2145,\n                2148\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                2586,\n                2589\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3044,\n                3047\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3096,\n                3099\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3348,\n                3351\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3409,\n                3412\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3585,\n                3588\n              ]\n            },\n            {\n              "text": "and",\n              "indices": [\n                3731,\n                3734\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/bcfda99ccfa86c54a40a8b8fa650b0c9df813475",\n      "sha": "bcfda99ccfa86c54a40a8b8fa650b0c9df813475",\n      "node_id": "MDY6Q29tbWl0MzY5NDUyNzI6YmNmZGE5OWNjZmE4NmM1NGE0MGE4YjhmYTY1MGIwYzlkZjgxMzQ3NQ==",\n      "html_url": "https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commit/bcfda99ccfa86c54a40a8b8fa650b0c9df813475",\n      "comments_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/bcfda99ccfa86c54a40a8b8fa650b0c9df813475/comments",\n      "commit": {\n        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/git/commits/bcfda99ccfa86c54a40a8b8fa650b0c9df813475",\n        "author": {\n          "date": "2023-08-14T14:31:09.000+05:30",\n          "name": "Srinivasan Kannan",\n          "email": "ka.shrinivaasan@gmail.com"\n        },\n        "committer": {\n          "date": "2023-08-14T14:31:09.000+05:30",\n          "name": "Srinivasan Kannan",\n          "email": "ka.shrinivaasan@gmail.com"\n        },\n        "message": "--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1444. (THEORY and FEATURE) Software Analytics and Kernel Analytics - Python script to parse asfer_syscall_trace.log for Filesystem prefetch VIRGO kernel analytics config - related to 1191,619,770,906,911,771,1192,1443 and all sections on Program Analysis, AsFer-USBmd-VIRGO-KingCobra FTrace kernel callgraph analysis - Valgrind-Callgrind-KCacheGrind-Flamegraph userspace callgraph analysis, SATURN control flow analysis of VIRGO linux kernel, Software Analytics, Pandemic model of Botnet-Defense adversarial Game theoretic security, Cybercrime analytics, Anti-Malware,Anti-Ransomware,Anti-Theft and Anti-Plagiarism tools, Filesystem Prefetch, KML, LAKE - 14 August 2023\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n1444.1 New Python script asfer_syscall_trace.py has been added to repository for parsing strace log for read() systemcalls captured from a process in asfer_syscall_trace.log (process_id 2206 - pulseaudio - in this example), get a set of frequently read filedescriptors and append a keyvalue pair \\"frequently_read_filedescriptors=<set of filedescriptors>\\" to /etc/virgo_kernel_analytics.conf read and published within VIRGO linux kernel by virgo_kernel_analytics.ko driver module.\\n1444.2 Parse logs for asfer_syscall_trace.py can be found in testlogs/asfer_syscall_trace.py.log.14August2023\\n1444.3 Sample /etc/virgo_kernel_analytics.conf config file (list of comma separated key-value pairs) for virgo_kernel_analytics.ko driver written earlier has also been added to repository.\\n1444.4 Suspicious syscall sequences could as well point to malware infection (Section 1192.10) apart from filesystem prefetch.\\n1444.5 Present implementation of asfer_syscall_trace.sh and asfer_syscall_trace.py is limited to read() system calls and could be extended to any other system calls including write(), Network Socket I/O and VIRGO system call internal loadbalancers (Example: function char* get_host_from_cloud_Loadtrack_mempool() - https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/virgo64-linux-github-code/blob/master/linux-kernel-extensions/virgo_malloc/virgo_malloc.c - which is supposed to return least loaded host based on load stats might benefit by reading /etc/virgo_kernel_analytics.conf key-value pairs for load stats set from /proc/loadavg , top or uptime commands data - but not implemented at present). Script https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/virgo64-linux-github-code/blob/master/virgo-python-src/VirgoPRGLoadBalancer_probab_calc.py computes expected load per host for pseudorandom loadbalancer (randomly route requests to any node).",\n        "tree": {\n          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/git/trees/51652f91c71934cadab60e0b2c6d10ed41b307dd",\n          "sha": "51652f91c71934cadab60e0b2c6d10ed41b307dd"\n        },\n        "comment_count": 0\n      },\n      "author": null,\n      "committer": null,\n      "parents": [\n        {\n          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commits/6b7fff038ca9614a5b7e0b650c7f58afcbe982c0",\n          "html_url": "https://github.com/shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code/commit/6b7fff038ca9614a5b7e0b650c7f58afcbe982c0",\n          "sha": "6b7fff038ca9614a5b7e0b650c7f58afcbe982c0"\n        }\n      ],\n      "repository": {\n        "id": 36945272,\n        "node_id": "MDEwOlJlcG9zaXRvcnkzNjk0NTI3Mg==",\n        "name": "asfer-github-code",\n        "full_name": "shrinivaasanka/asfer-github-code",\n        "private": false,\n        "owner": {\n          "login": "shrinivaasanka",\n          "id": 8086981,\n          "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjgwODY5ODE=",\n          "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8086981?v=4",\n          "gravatar_id": "",\n          "url": "https://api.github.com/users/shrinivaasanka",\n          "html_url": "https://gi